MOSAIC MULTICULTURAL CONNECTIONS 
Akol’s Story
Armidale, NSW  |  2026 
Eight Years in the Waiting
Akol, Armidale NSW 
The  application went  in  2017. Akol Manyang Nyinde Koor was  still  a  teenager  in  South 
Sudan, growing up in a household held together by her uncle's wages sent from Australia. 
He had been paying for food,  school  fees,  medicine,  and  rent  from  the  other  side  of  the 
world for years. When he applied for his nieces and nephews to join him, they waited. And 
kept waiting. 
They arrived on March 11, 2025. Eight years after the paperwork was lodged. 
"When we reached the Sydney airport, we were all looking everywhere," Akol says. "We 
were like, this is Australia. Back home it is just a small town. It's called a city, but it's a town. 
And then here, tarmac roads everywhere." 
Akol grew up knowing she wanted to be a doctor. She had watched her uncle carry the 
weight of an entire family from abroad, had felt what it meant to depend on one person's 
generosity for survival, and had understood early that education was the way to change that 
equation. Australia, in her mind, was where she was going to study properly, access real 
facilities, and build something that could stand on its own. 

