What she   did  not   know    until    she   arrived was   how   much the  visa  category she   came in  on 
would shape her first months here. The Orphan Relative Visa, subclass 117, does not carry 
access to Centrelink payments for adults. Akol and her siblings were over eighteen by the time 
their    application was   approved, which meant the  government considered them   capable of 
supporting themselves. Their uncle had agreed to take responsibility for them, and he did. But 
the financial pressure was real and immediate. 
There was another detail Akol had not factored into the plan. She arrived pregnant. 
It was Cholhok Mathiang, a family connection who had worked with Mosaic, who brought 
Akol and her siblings to meet Madan, their caseworker, and that first meeting set the tone for 
everything that followed. 
Akol with her caseworker at Mosaic Multicultural Connections, Armidale 
"Everyone kept telling us, getting jobs is not easy when you have just arrived in Australia," 
Akol says. "But with Mosaic, everything was made simple." 
Madan found a horticulture farm   in  the  region with   casual harvest positions available. He 
organised mock interviews, let them use the office computers to sit the real interview online, 
and  kept  every  question  answered.  When  the  email  came  through  confirming  all  three 
siblings had been offered positions, the household did not sleep. "We kept checking what 
time it was," Akol says. "At six in the morning we were already awake, showered, had our 
breakfast. It was our first time in our lives to work." 
That was a year ago. Akol is still working at the farm. 

