What the farm has meant to her goes beyond a pay cheque. When she was heavily pregnant, 
she was not put on a roster and left to manage. Supervisors noticed when she was on her feet 
too long. They told her to sit. When she felt dizzy, they sent her for water. After her baby was 
born  and  childcare  arrangements  occasionally  fell  through,  there  was  no  pressure,  no 
performance review, no quiet disappointment. She would text in the morning to say she was 
not coming, and the response was always the same: that's alright. 
"They acted like my family. I don't know if we would ever get the chance to see 
people like them."  — Akol Koor
Working through job applications at Mosaic's Armidale office 
Mosaic kept moving through that period too. Madan worked through Centrelink with Akol to 
ensure her baby was registered for the payments she was entitled to, even though Akol herself 
was not eligible for personal support under her visa. Photo identification was sorted. Learner 
driver  permits  were  obtained.  First  Aid  certification  was  completed,  including  CPR,  fire 
response,  and  choking  procedures  that  Akol  now  applies  as  a  mother.  "If  my  baby  picks 
something up and puts it in his mouth, I know what to do," she says. "I used to think you had 
to put your hand in and pull it out." 
She says it lightly, the way people speak about things that used to frighten them and no longer 
do. 
Akol's uncle, who spent years sending money home to keep his family fed and educated, now 
talks to the siblings about discipline. About being kind, being honest, giving people a proper 
hearing.  He  is  happy.  He  says  so  plainly,  and  Akol  repeats  it  with  the quiet  satisfaction  of 
someone who knows what it cost him to get them here. 

